              "value": "(a). The Text is that of the Apocalypse intercalated with extracts from the com-\nmentary of Berengaudus (Patrologia Latina xvii). There is a startling likeness\nin minute detail to the text of MS. C (see Related Material).\nf. 1. Inc. prol. apocalipsis iohannis apostoli.\nApocalipsis iohannis tot habet - intelligence (Jerome's Prologue). Expl.\nprol. Inc. Apoc. Iohannis.\nApocalypsis Iesu Christi - loquebatur mecum (i. 12).\nExpositio. Apocalypsis reuelatio interpretatur (end of f. 1). 1b. Et conuersus\nuidi - septem sunt ecclesie. Et angelo ecclesie ephesi scribe. Et angelo. i. (ii.)\neccl. Smirne scribe. Et angelo. ii. (iii.) pergami eccl. scr. Et angelo. iii. (iv.) eccl.\nthyathyre scr. Et angelo. iiii. (v.) eccl. Sardis scr. Et angelo. v. (vi.) eccl. phyla-\ndelphye scr. Et angelo. vi. (vii.) eccl. laodicie. scribe, vii. (viii.) qui habet aurem -\necclesiis.\nThe angels have been wrongly numbered by the original scribe, and the\ncorrections interlined (here indicated in parentheses). Exactly the same thing\nhas happened in the Thompson MS.\nExpositio. Per septem ecclesias una catholica etc. - Tu es sacerdos etc.\n2a. Inc. textus uisionis secunde. Post hec uidi - uiuenti in sec. sec. In the rubric\nwe have Expos. tercie, but at the end of the page is another rubric.\nProxima pars sequens est tota secunde uisionis.\n3a. (secunde) - creata sunt (iv. fin.).\n3b. Textus tercie uisionis. Et uidi et ecce in medio throni - et fulgura et terre\nmotus (v. 6-viii. 5: viii. 6, 7 are omitted: so too in the Thompson MS.).\n9b. Textus quarte uisionis. Et secundus angelus - sexaginta sex (viii. 8-xiii. 18).\nOn 17a the hand suddenly becomes enormous, with 11 lines to a column\ninstead of 20: but 17b is normal again.\nAt 20a is a change to a larger and blacker script, with 18 lines to a full\ncolumn.\n21a. Textus quinte uisionis. Et uidi et ecce agnus - die ac nocte in sec. sec.\n(xiv. 1-xx. 10).\nThe title quarte continues in the rubrics on 23-25.\nAt 26 quinte reappears.\n30. After nondum acceperunt xvii. 12c-18 omitted.\n30b. xviii. 1-10, iudicium eius. xviii. 11-20 omitted.\n37a. Inc. textus sexte uisionis. Et uidi thronum - mors secunda (xx. 11-xxi. 9).\n38a. Inc. libri septeni uisionis textus (so also in the Thompson MS.). Et uenit\nunus - omnibus uobis. Amen. (xxi. 10-xxii. 21: omitting xxi. 20-27.)\nExpositio ends on 39a: continuo angelum adorare uoluit, and there is no\nexposition of the last section.\n39b. Expl. liber appocalipsis domini.\nThis is in a large hand, on ground of red hatching between green lines.\n\n(b). Short list of pictures.\n1. f. ii b. Full page. Benedictine monk painting an image of the Virgin and Child.\n2. 1. St John on Patmos. Bull. Pl. XIV.\n3. 1b. Vision of the Son of Man (i. 12-20).\n4. 2. The Majesty (iv. 1-9).\n5. 2b. Who will open the Book? (v. 1-5).\n6. 3. The Majesty (iv. 10, 11).\n7. 3b. The Lamb (v. 6).\n8. 4. The Lamb takes the Book. Pl. XVI.\n9. 4b. First seal (vi. 1, 2).\n10. 5. Second seal (vi. 3). Pl. XVIII.\n11. 5b. Third seal (vi. 5). PI. XIXa.\n12. 6. Fourth seal (vi. 7). Pl. XIXb.\n[The horsemen of the Apocalypse.]\n13. 6b. Fifth seal (vi. 9).\n14. 7. Sixth seal (vi. 12).\n15. 7b. The four angels and the winds (vii. 1).\n16. 8. The great multitude (vii. 9).\n17. 8b. The trumpets given (viii. 2).\n18. 9. The censer cast into earth (viii. 5), and First trumpet.\n19. 9b. The second, third and fourth trumpets (viii. 8).\n20. 10. The Eagle. Fifth trumpet (viii. 13).\n21. 10b. Locusts and Abaddon (ix. 1).\n22. 11. Sixth trumpet. Angels in Euphrates (ix. 13).\n23. 11b. The horsemen (ix. 17).\n23a. 11b. added lower margin: Antichrist and the witnesses. Pl. XXII.\n24. 12. The great angel (x. 1). Pl. XXII.\n24a. 12. added margin: Antichrist slays the witnesses. His miracles. Pl. XXII.\n25. 12b. Measuring the temple (x. 11).\n25a. 12b. added: Antichrist enthroned.\n26. 13. The witnesses (xi. 3).\n26a. 13 added: Antichrist persecutes. Is overthrown. Pl. XXII.\n27. 13b. The witnesses slain (xi. 7).\n28. 14. They are raised (xi. 9).\n29. 14b. Seventh Trumpet. Adoration (xi. 15).\n30. 15. The Dragon and the Woman (xi. 19).\n31. 15b. Michael and the Dragon (xii. 7).\n32. 16. Flight of the Woman (xii. 13).\n33. 16b. The Dragon pursues (xii. 15).\n34. 17. The seed of the Woman fight the Dragon (xii. 17).\n35. 17b. The Dragon gives sceptre to the Beast (xiii. 1). Pl. XXV.\n36. 18. The Dragon adored (xiii. 3).\n37. 18b. The Beast adored (xiii. 4).\n38. 19. The Beast persecutes the saints (xiii. 7).\n39. 19b. The False Prophet. The Beast adored (xiii. 11).\n40. 20. False Prophet. Beast adored (xiii. 14).\n41. 20b. Similar (xiii. 16).\n42. 21. Lamb on Mount Sion (xiv. 1). Pal. Soc. ii. 195.\n43. 21b. Adoration (xiv. 2).\n44. 11. Angel proclaiming woe (xiv. 6).\n45. 22b. \"Babylon is fallen\" (xiv. 8).\n46. 23. Third Angel (xiv. 9).\n47. 23b. Beati mortui (xiv. 13).\n48. 24. Harvest of earth (xiv. 14).\n49. 24b. Vintage of earth (xiv. 17).\n50. 25. Vintage trodden (xiv. 19). Pl. XXVI.\n51. 25b. The Harpers (xv. 1).\n52. 26. Vials given (by the Lion) (xv. 5).\n53. 26b. First Vial (xvi. 1).\n54. 27. Second Vial, Third Vial (xvi. 3). Pl. XXVI.\n55. 27b. Fourth Vial (xvi. 8).\n56. 28. Fifth Vial, Sixth Vial (xvi. 10).\n57. 28b. The Frogs (xvi. 13).\n58. 29. Seventh Vial (xvi. 17).\n59. 29b. The Woman on the Hill (xvii. 1).\n60. 30. The Woman on the Beast (xvii. 3). Pl. XXVIII.\n61. 30b. Ruin of Babylon (xviii. 1).\n62. 31. The Millstone (xviii. 21). Pl. XXVIII.\n63. 31b. Adoration. The Woman tormented (xix. 1).\n64. 32. Marriage supper of the Lamb (xix. 6).\n65. 32b. John would worship the angel (xix. 9).\n66. 33. Christ on the horse. In the winepress (xix. 11).\n67. 33b. The Birds summoned (xix. 17).\n68. 34. Defeat of the Beast (xix. 19).\n69. 34b. The Beast and False Prophet pursued (xix. 20).\n70. 35. Dragon cast into pit (xx. 1).\n71. 35b. First Judgment (xx. 4).\n72. 36 Dragon comes out of hell (xx. 7).\n73. 36b. Hell mouth (xx. 9).\n74. 37. The Judgment (xx. 11).\n75. 37b. New Jerusalem (xxi. 2).\n76. 38. The City again (xxi. 9).\n77. 38b. The River of Life (xxii. 1).\n78. 39. \"Worship God\" (xxii. 8).\n79. 39b John adores Christ (xxii. 10). Burlington Fine Arts Catalogue, 1908. Pl. 73.\n80. 40. St Christopher. Burlington F. A. Pl. 73.\n81. 40b. St John preaching; converts Drusiana. Baptizes Drusiana.\n82. 41. St John before the proconsul. Pl. XXX. Embarks for Rome.\n83. 41b. St John before Domitian (2 scenes).\n84. 42. St John in the caldron of oil. Banished to Patmos.\n85. 42b. Sails to Patmos. Received at Ephesus.\n86. 43. Raises Drusiana. Atticus and Eugenius, scene 1.\n87. 43b. Atticus and Eugenius, scene 2. Destroys Diana's temple.\n88. 44. The criminals drink poison. John drinks the poison.\n89. 44b. Aristodemus and John. Aristodemus raises the criminals.\n90. 45. St John's last mass. He dies.\n91. 45b. St George raised by the Virgin to fight Julian. Slays Julian.\n92. 46. Theophilus sells his soul to the devil (2 scenes). Pl. XXXIII.\n93. 46b. He prays to the Virgin. She intercedes with Christ.\n94. 47. She wrests the bond from the devil. She gives it to Theophilus.\n95. 47b. St John writing the Apocalypse on Patmos.\n96. 48. Virgin and Child. Lady de Quincy. Pl. XXXIV.\n97. 48b. Cherub or Seraph with inscribed wings, as in Arundel 83, etc.\n98. 49. Noli me tangere.\n99. 49b. St Laurence,\n100. 50. St Katherine.\n101. 50b. St Margaret.\n102. 51. St Edmund shot with arrows.\n103. 51b. The Crucifixion, a noteworthy composition.\n104. 52. An Archbishop.\n105. 52b. Another: traced from the preceding.\n106. 53. Allegory of Christian life, with copious explanatory labels.\n107. 53b. The Holy Face.\nNos. 97-107 are tinted drawings which specially recall St Albans' work. But all the\npictures which follow the Apocalypse have something of this character. Most of them tell\ntheir own tale, but the allegorical one, f. 53, may be conveniently annotated.\nLow down on L. is the principal figure, a lady seated in a rich chair: a dove is on the\nback of it. She holds the shield of the Trinity, which is inscribed in French. The\ninscriptions referring to this part of the picture are: Par la dame est signifie repentant,\npar le columbe li sainz espiriz qui espunt la sainte escriture, par lescu la foi.\nIn C. is a tree: at top of it a cock; at the root, at the lady's foot, a viper: on R. a\nman in a linen hood chopping at the tree with an axe. Above him a cock-footed devil\nshooting an arrow to L. On L. of the tree trunk a cloud of flies. In the upper R. corner\nan angel flying down with a leaf-shaped fly-flapper. In the upper L. corner an angel with\na sword. At the bottom of the picture, water.\nThe inscriptions are:\npar larbre cest mund.\npar le coc en le arbre est signifie le precheur qui defule et le preie a despire.\npar le colouere agaitant al talon le desturbement que le diable se aforce afaire as leals\nal issue del alme.\npar la coigne la sentence del iugement ou le prechement del euuangile.\npar le diable setant, les suggestions del diable.\npar les musches les ueines pensees qui desturbent le repentant orant.\npar langle remuant les musches od le muscher le aid de langle qui est gardain de\nchescun home.\npar langle od lespee qui est pres del haterel: la destresce del deuin iugement.\npar leuue: deuine escriture la quele le precheur demustre."
